output:
{
  "title": "Three self-hosting bugs whose symptoms pointed the wrong way",
  "purpose": "A write-up for anyone self-hosting this thing who hits one of these three failures. Each symptom names the wrong subsystem, so the point of the page is to shorten the gap between what you see and where the fault actually is. No answers needed — read it, and check the fourth section before you add a page stylesheet.",
  "resumeHint": "If you are hitting `unauthorized` right now, jump to the first finding and check the base URL your client was built with before you touch keys.",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "widget": "summary",
      "props": {
        "title": "What this is",
        "lead": "Self-hosting exposed three bugs that had lived comfortably in a single-deployment world. What they have in common is not the code — one is auth, one is a docs string, one is config loading — it is that each presents as a problem in the layer above its cause. Read the symptom column and you go the wrong way every time.",
        "points": [
          "`unauthorized` on every call: reads as a signing bug, is actually the client talking to the wrong host.",
          "A `claude mcp add` command that arrives as one joined line: reads as a copy-paste or shell problem, is actually a line continuation eaten by a template literal.",
          "A timeout that matches nothing you configured: reads as a bug in the waiting code, is actually a config loader silently swallowing an unparseable value.",
          "A bonus fourth: preview inputs rendering tiny and uppercase — a page stylesheet redefining a class the shared stylesheet owns. Now guarded by a test, because eyes are not a method."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "comparison",
      "props": {
        "title": "Symptom, and where it sends you",
        "columns": ["What you see", "Where it points", "Where the fault is"],
        "rows": [
          [
            "`unauthorized` on every MCP call, including the first",
            "Key generation, clock skew, signature algorithm",
            "The client's hardcoded default base URL — it is signing requests to a host that has never seen your key"
          ],
          [
            "The `claude mcp add` command on /start is one long line, backslashes missing",
            "Your terminal, your clipboard, the docs being sloppy",
            "A lone trailing `\\` inside a template literal in the page source, consumed as a line continuation"
          ],
          [
            "A wait that ends at a duration you never set",
            "The await/polling logic, the tunnel, an upstream timeout",
            "The env loader parsed nothing, took the default, and said nothing about it"
          ],
          [
            "Every input inside a block preview is small and uppercase",
            "The block renderer's field markup",
            "A page stylesheet defining `.label`, a class the shared stylesheet already owns"
          ]
        ],
        "caption": "Three of these were found by following the wrong column first."
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "findings",
      "props": {
        "title": "The three, in detail",
        "findings": [
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "The MCP client shipped with somebody else's address baked in",
            "detail": "The client file hardcoded this deployment as its default base URL. A self-hoster who followed /start by hand installed a client that signed every request to the origin deployment using a key that origin had never issued. The result is `unauthorized` on the first call and every call after it — which is exactly what a broken signature looks like, so that is where you go, and you can burn an afternoon regenerating keys that were never wrong. The fix is that the instance now substitutes its own URL when it serves the file, the way /cli already did: a client downloaded from your deployment talks to your deployment. If you installed a client before this, reinstall it rather than editing keys."
          },
          {
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "A trailing backslash inside a template literal is a line continuation",
            "detail": "The multi-line `claude mcp add` command on /start was built in a template literal with a lone `\\` at the end of each line. In a template literal that backslash escapes the newline: both the newline and the backslash disappear, and the reader gets one joined line with no indication anything was removed. Nothing throws, nothing lints, and the command still looks plausible. To emit a literal trailing backslash you have to write `\\\\`, or assemble the lines and join them yourself."
          },
          {
            "tone": "danger",
            "title": "The config loader fell back silently, in a loader whose whole posture is refusing loudly",
            "detail": "`MAX_AWAIT_MS=90s` is not a number. The loader parsed it, got nothing usable, quietly substituted the default, and started up clean — so the running deployment used a value the operator had not chosen while the environment plainly said otherwise. This was the one loader in the codebase that exists to refuse bad input loudly, which is precisely why nobody suspected it. It refuses now: an unparseable numeric env var is a startup failure that names the variable and the value it could not read."
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "widget": "annotated-code",
      "props": {
        "title": "The two you can reproduce in ten seconds",
        "notes": [
          {
            "note": "The continuation. Both the newline and the backslash are gone from the output — this is a joined string, not a three-line command.",
            "language": "js",
            "code": "const cmd = `claude mcp add sirdocalot \\\n  --transport http \\\n  https://your-host.example/mcp`;\n\n// cmd === \"claude mcp add sirdocalot   --transport http   https://your-host.example/mcp\""
          },
          {
            "note": "What you have to write instead if the reader is meant to see backslashes. Doubling is the minimum; joining an array is the version that survives the next edit.",
            "language": "js",
            "code": "const cmd = [\n  \"claude mcp add sirdocalot\",\n  \"  --transport http\",\n  \"  https://your-host.example/mcp\",\n].join(\" \\\\\\n\");"
          },
          {
            "note": "The silent fallback. `Number(\"90s\")` is NaN, `??` does not catch NaN, and even when the guard is written correctly the default is returned without a word.",
            "language": "js",
            "code": "// before\nconst ms = Number(process.env.MAX_AWAIT_MS) || DEFAULT_AWAIT_MS;\n\n// after\nconst raw = process.env.MAX_AWAIT_MS;\nif (raw !== undefined) {\n  const ms = Number(raw);\n  if (!Number.isFinite(ms)) {\n    throw new Error(`MAX_AWAIT_MS: expected a number of milliseconds, got ${JSON.stringify(raw)}`);\n  }\n}"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "kind": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "The fourth thing: a guard, not a fix"
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "The gallery page defined its own `.label`. `.label` is what the block renderer calls a field label, so every input inside every preview rendered uppercase and tiny — a symptom in the renderer, a cause in a stylesheet nobody was looking at. That is the second time a page stylesheet has quietly redefined a shared class, and both times it was caught by somebody noticing it looked wrong. Noticing is not a method; it does not scale and it does not run in CI."
    },
    {
      "kind": "prose",
      "text": "There is now a test asserting that no page stylesheet redefines a class the shared stylesheet defines. The test was proved to bite before it was trusted: the old gallery `.label` was reintroduced, the test failed, and only then was it removed again. A guard that has never been seen to fail is a guard you are guessing about."
    },
    {
      "kind": "callout",
      "tone": "warn",
      "text": "If you add a page-scoped stylesheet, expect this test to be the thing that stops you. Namespace the class or extend the shared definition; do not redefine the name."
    },
    {
      "widget": "key-facts",
      "props": {
        "title": "If you are debugging one of these right now",
        "facts": [
          {
            "key": "`unauthorized` on every call",
            "value": "Print the base URL your installed MCP client is using before you look at the key. If it is not your host, reinstall the client from your deployment."
          },
          {
            "key": "Setup command looks mangled",
            "value": "View source on /start. If the command is one line there too, it is the template literal, not your terminal."
          },
          {
            "key": "Timeout you did not set",
            "value": "Restart with your env in place. Any unparseable numeric var now fails startup by name. If it starts clean, the value was read."
          },
          {
            "key": "Preview styling wrong after a page CSS change",
            "value": "Run the stylesheet-collision test. It names the class and the file."
          },
          {
            "key": "General shape",
            "value": "All four symptoms surfaced one layer above their cause. When the obvious layer looks correct on inspection, check what was substituted for you silently."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
